Let’s understand what a procedure is in Oracle Database, in very simple language 👇

🧩 Definition:
A procedure in Oracle Database is a named block of PL/SQL code that performs a specific task.

You can think of it like a function in programming — it is written once and can be called many times whenever you need that task.

💡 Real-life Example (Simple way):
Imagine you work in a bank.

Every time a customer deposits money, you:

Check account number

Add amount to balance

Print confirmation message

You can write all this logic once inside a procedure called deposit_money.
Then, whenever a deposit happens, you simply call that procedure instead of writing the same steps again.

⚙️ Basic Syntax of a Procedure
CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE procedure_name
IS
BEGIN
   -- Your PL/SQL statements go here
END;
/
🧠 Example 1: Simple Procedure
Let’s create a procedure to print “Hello” message.

CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE say_hello
IS
BEGIN
   DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('Hello, welcome to Oracle!');
END;
/
👉 To execute this procedure:

EXECUTE say_hello;
Output:

Hello, welcome to Oracle!
🧮 Example 2: Procedure with Parameters
Let’s make it more useful — a procedure that prints employee details.

CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE show_employee(
   emp_id IN NUMBER
)
IS
   emp_name VARCHAR2(50);
BEGIN
   SELECT name INTO emp_name FROM employees WHERE id = emp_id;
   DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('Employee Name: ' || emp_name);
EXCEPTION
   WHEN NO_DATA_FOUND THEN
      DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('Employee not found!');
END;
/
👉 To run it:

EXECUTE show_employee(101);
📦 Key Points:
Feature	Description
Stored in DB	Procedure is saved permanently in Oracle Database.
Reusability	Can be called many times by different users/programs.
Parameters	Can accept input (IN), give output (OUT), or both (IN OUT).
Error Handling	You can handle exceptions inside procedure using EXCEPTION block.
Improves Performance	Stored once and runs faster than sending SQL again and again.
🧩 Types of Parameters
Type	Purpose	Example
IN	Input only	emp_id IN NUMBER
OUT	Output only	emp_name OUT VARCHAR2
IN OUT	Input + Output	total_salary IN OUT NUMBER
🧰 Example 3: Procedure with IN and OUT Parameters
CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE get_bonus(
   emp_id IN NUMBER,
   bonus OUT NUMBER
)
IS
   salary NUMBER;
BEGIN
   SELECT sal INTO salary FROM employees WHERE id = emp_id;
   bonus := salary * 0.10;
END;
/
👉 To execute:

DECLARE
   b NUMBER;
BEGIN
   get_bonus(101, b);
   DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('Bonus = ' || b);
END;
/
✅ Advantages of Using Procedures
Code reusability

Centralized business logic

Faster performance

Security (access control via GRANT EXECUTE)

Easier maintenance

